In Ladybird, the properties -webkit-box-align, -webkit-box-pack,
-webkit-box-flex, and -webkit-box-ordinal-group are now properties
of their own, which lay out only a -webkit-box, while align-items,
justify-content, flex-grow, and order lay out only a flex container.
Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit
26.5, all three engines do the same, and agree on the computed values
(align-items stays normal when -webkit-box-align is set, e.g.).

Blink keeps them as the BoxAlign, BoxPack, BoxFlex, and BoxOrdinalGroup
style fields, and FlexLayoutAlgorithm reads them only for a -webkit-box
(is_webkit_box_), in ResolvedJustifyContent() and the item alignment
switch on BoxAlign(). Gecko keeps them in nsStyleXUL as mBoxAlign,
mBoxPack, mBoxFlex, and mBoxOrdinalGroup, which nsFlexContainerFrame
reads only when IsLegacyWebkitBox(): ConvertLegacyStyleToAlignItems()
and ConvertLegacyStyleToJustifyContent() map them, mBoxFlex sets
both flex grow and flex shrink, and OrderingPropertyForIter() orders
the items by BoxOrdinalGroup. WebKit lays out a -webkit-box with
RenderDeprecatedFlexibleBox, which reads its own -webkit-box properties.

The grammar follows the two engines that agree: Chrome and WebKit
accept any number for -webkit-box-flex, where Firefox rejects a negative
one, and they reject an ordinal group of 0, which Firefox accepts.
The keyword mappings for start and end follow Gecko’s flex-start and
flex-end; Blink uses the flow-relative start and end, which differ only
with “-webkit-box-direction: reverse”, which Ladybird doesn’t support.
